## Ⅲ. COMPUTATIONAL MODELS (Execution Layer)

These READ doctrine and EXECUTE based on it.

### Model Categories

```
ORCHESTRATORS (Ω) — Control models
    Ω₁ SOVEREIGN_TICK         Controls beat lifecycle
    Ω₂ SPHERICAL_INTEGRATION  Couples all planes
    Ω₃ SWARM_CORE             Routes agent work
    Ω₄ FULL_GOVERNANCE        Manages approvals
    Ω₅ CONSTITUTIONAL_LAW     Enforces RECITAL_PLUS_ONE
    Ω₆ NEURAL_CORE_MESH       Maintains mesh coherence
    Ω₇ LIVING_DOCUMENT        Manages D1-D10 lifecycle
    Ω₈ FRONTEND_COMMAND       Routes UI commands

RUNTIME (ρ) — Execution models
    ρ₁-ρ₂₃ (23 models)
    Examples: HEARTBEAT-CORE, MEMORY-TEMPLE-STATE,
              AUTONOMOUS-ANALYST, SACRED-MATHEMATICS

INTERFACE (υ) — World-facing models
    υ₁-υ₁₂ (12 models)
    Examples: MEDINA-COMMAND-CENTER, MEMORY-NAVIGATION,
              DRONE-SIM-WORLD, COMPANION-CONSOLE

DOCUMENT (δ) — Living document models
    δ₁-δ₈ (8 models)
    Examples: STRATEGIST, BUILDER, ANALYST,
              MEMORY-CURATOR, PROJECTION

SOVEREIGN (ν) — Macro node models
    ν₁-ν₁₂ (12 models)
    N1-N12 hierarchy (Core to Archive)

CODEX (𓃭𓆣𓇯𓂋) — Autonomous organisms
    ALPHA (𓃭) Compression
    BETA (𓆣) Mutation
    GAMMA (𓇯) Pattern
    DELTA (𓂋) Translation
```

### How Computational Models Work

```
computational_model.execute():
    1. READ assigned doctrine
    2. EXTRACT rules from doctrine
    3. RECEIVE input (CPL message)
    4. VALIDATE input against rules
    5. TRANSFORM input per doctrine
    6. OUTPUT result (CPL message)
    7. LOG to lineage

THE MODEL IS WHAT THE DOCTRINE SAYS IT IS.
CHANGE DOCTRINE → CHANGE MODEL BEHAVIOR.
```

